message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Quantum Nematode: simulated C. elegans graded against real-worm behaviour, and what the connectome contributes"
authors:
  - family-names: Zaharia
    given-names: Chris Julian
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6849-8081"
repository-code: "https://github.com/SyntheticBrains/nematode"
url: "https://github.com/SyntheticBrains/nematode"
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.8.0
date-released: "2026-11-28"
keywords:
  - C. elegans
  - connectome
  - computational neuroscience
  - reinforcement learning
  - embodied learning
  - evolutionary optimisation
  - quantum machine learning
  - simulation
abstract: >-
  A closed-loop sensory-motor simulation platform for asking how faithfully a
  simulated C. elegans reproduces the worm's standard behaviours, graded against
  real-worm data, and what the real connectome contributes to that fidelity. A
  simulated worm forages on bacterial lawns, evades predators and navigates
  thermal and oxygen gradients while a pluggable brain architecture —
  feed-forward, recurrent, spiking, reservoir, quantum, hybrid, GA-evolved or
  constrained to the real 302-neuron C. elegans connectome — is trained by
  learning, evolution or fitting to real-worm data. The architecture families are
  controllers compared on fidelity; quantum circuits are one family. The grader,
  released as the wormbench package, grades 1-s tracks on standard on-lawn assays
  (roaming and dwelling, lawn leaving, kinematics and activity, local search after
  food removal and butanone chemotaxis) against bands built from the spread
  between real recording dates.
